    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "paper question",
      "narration_text": "The paper asks a fundamental question: how do researchers understand and classify uncertainty when they study environmental change?",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B05_Question",
        "visual_intent": "The paper's question.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.3,
            "event": "question lands, terracotta ?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 8.92
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "context",
      "narration_text": "Uncertainty is built into environmental research. Scientists studying climate, ecosystems and risk work with incomplete knowledge, imperfect models, assumptions, and unpredictable natural processes.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B06_Sources",
        "visual_intent": "Four sources of uncertainty around environmental research.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.5,
            "event": "incomplete knowledge"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.62,
            "event": "imperfect models"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.72,
            "event": "assumptions"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.82,
            "event": "unpredictable processes"
          }
        ]
      },
      "qc": {
        "sparse_by_design": true,
        "sparse_reason": "build-up beat: items land one per phrase"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.95
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "the problem",
      "narration_text": "Many frameworks and taxonomies describe uncertainty, but their terms aren't consistent. The same term can mean different things across disciplines, and different terms can describe similar ideas.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B07_Terms",
        "visual_intent": "Same term, different meanings; different terms, same idea.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.1,
            "event": "many frameworks"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.5,
            "event": "one term splits"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.78,
            "event": "many terms merge"
          }
        ]
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.35
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "act": "method",
      "narration_text": "To investigate, Bevan reviewed one hundred fifty six uncertainty frameworks: how each classifies uncertainty, its conceptual foundations, and how its categories relate.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B08_Review",
        "visual_intent": "156 frameworks reviewed on three questions.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.1,
            "event": "156-square grid fills"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.55,
            "event": "classification · foundations · relations"
          }
        ]
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.41
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "act": "finding",
      "narration_text": "A major finding: the frameworks themselves contain ambiguities. For example, the line between uncertainty from limited knowledge and uncertainty from inherent variability is not always drawn the same way.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B09_Line",
        "visual_intent": "Where is the line between knowledge and variability?",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.1,
            "event": "frameworks contain ambiguities"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.5,
            "event": "three frameworks draw the line in different places"
          }
        ]
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.44
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "act": "argument",
      "narration_text": "So the author argues that one universal framework may not be realistic. It may be more useful to understand how researchers and practitioners interpret uncertainty in their actual work.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B10_Context",
        "visual_intent": "From one universal framework to uncertainty in context.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.1,
            "event": "universal framework, crossed"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.55,
            "event": "uncertainty in context of practice"
          }
        ]
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.78
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "act": "insight",
      "narration_text": "For my research, this means environmental uncertainty can't be captured by one confidence score. Before asking A I for a confidence percentage, we need to know what type of uncertainty exists, what caused it, and what that score represents.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B11_Score",
        "visual_intent": "One score is not enough: three questions first.",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.1,
            "event": "a single confidence score, questioned"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.62,
            "event": "what type?"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.75,
            "event": "what caused it?"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.87,
            "event": "what does it represent?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "qc": {
        "sparse_by_design": true,
        "sparse_reason": "build-up beat: items land one per phrase"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.17
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "act": "insight",
      "narration_text": "That supports building a contextual uncertainty classification for A I based environmental E S G assessments, rather than blindly relying on a single score.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
